  step (2): largest Gamma(eta') - Gamma(eta) over 20000 random cases with eta' <= eta, weights of sum <= 1: 0.000e+00 (must be <= 0);
            strictly negative in 19966 cases (it is 0 when all books are 0 and the weights sum to 1)
  8 random small games, c in {0.5, 1, 2}, 9 rounds, all adversaries and all minimisers: largest |S_t(B)| / bound = 0.7287 (must be <= 1)
  constants: 1 + ln 2 = 1.6931 <= 1.70;  (1 + ln 2)^2 = 2.867

Part 6. Proposition 6.6
  steps (3) to (5): rho = 1 - mu^2/(8e); (1+mu)/(2e) <= 1/e; sqrt(r^2-2mu r+1) <= r - mu/2 for r >= 1/mu; e^u <= 1+u+(e/2)u^2 on [-1,1]; C(mu) = (1/lambda) ln(8e e^(1/e)/mu^2), for 0 < mu <= 1: True
  C(1) = 18.742, C(0.5) = 52.56, C(0.082) = 560.2; C(mu0) < 0 for mu0 > 5.605, for instance C(6) = -0.1234
  (so the statement needs mu0 <= 1, or at least C(mu0) >= 0, in the degenerate case where all books vanish)
  drift argument on the walk r -> r+1 with probability (1-mu)/2, r -> r-1 otherwise (from 0: to 1),
  which has E[r_t^2 | past] = r_{t-1}^2 - 2 mu r_{t-1} + 1 and steps of size 1:
     mu     max_n E r_n   C(mu)     max_n E exp(lambda r_n)   8e e^(1/e)/mu^2
    0.05      10.0145    1026.29         1.1014                 12566.4
    0.20       2.6000     181.21         1.1050                   785.4
    0.50       1.2500      52.56         1.1247                   125.7
    1.00       1.0000      18.74         1.2019                    31.4

